The factory produces \(50\) candies per hour and works \(10\) hours per day.

1. **Candies produced per day**
\[
50\ \frac{\text{candies}}{\text{hour}} \times 10\ \frac{\text{hours}}{\text{day}}
= (50\times 10)\ \frac{\text{candies}}{\text{day}}
= 500\ \frac{\text{candies}}{\text{day}}
\]

2. **Number of days to produce 4000 candies**
\[
\text{days}=\frac{4000\ \text{candies}}{500\ \frac{\text{candies}}{\text{day}}}
= \left(\frac{4000}{500}\right)\ \text{days}
= 8\ \text{days}
\]

\boxed{8}

Justin’s running speed is given by “2 blocks in 1.5 minutes.”

### 1) Convert the time to a fraction
\[
1.5=\frac{15}{10}=\frac{3}{2}
\]

### 2) Compute speed (blocks per minute)
\[
\text{speed}=\frac{2\ \text{blocks}}{\frac{3}{2}\ \text{minutes}}
=2\cdot\frac{2}{3}
=\frac{4}{3}\ \text{blocks/min}
\]

### 3) Compute time to run 8 blocks
\[
\text{time}=\frac{\text{distance}}{\text{speed}}
=\frac{8}{\frac{4}{3}\ \text{blocks/min}}
=8\cdot\frac{3}{4}
\]

### 4) Simplify
\[
8\cdot\frac{3}{4}=\left(\frac{8}{4}\right)\cdot 3=2\cdot 3=6
\]

\boxed{6}
